What was taken, and what was not
--------------------------------

TAKEN (identifiers, flags and structure only):

  - test case names, verbatim, so the two suites can be diffed;
  - the preprocessor symbol names active in each translation unit
    (ENABLE_*, TUNNEL_*, DSR_*, ENCAP*, IS_BPF_*, ENCRYPT*);
  - the ASSIGN_CONFIG global names each unit sets;
  - the include graph (which datapath object and which library modules a unit
    pulls in, which shared test header defines a case);
  - which of the PKTGEN / SETUP / CHECK stages each case defines, and which
    datapath entrypoint its SETUP stage tail-calls;
  - counts.

  Additionally, the address / MAC / port constants of bpf/tests/pktgen.h are
  reproduced value-for-value in tests/fixtures/addresses.toml, so that ported
  expectations remain numerically comparable with the reference during
  bring-up. Those are constant tables, whose direct copying docs/licensing.md
  permits with attribution; this file is that attribution.

NOT TAKEN:

  - no C source, no macro, no function body, no assertion expression;
  - no packet-builder call sequence;
  - no expected byte value, checksum literal or golden byte table (in
    particular, none of the scapy-generated tables in bpf/tests/scapy/*.py or
    bpf/tests/output/scapy_bytes.h — flowsdn regenerates equivalent packets
    with its own Rust builder);
  - no part of the Go driver in bpf/tests/bpftest/ (Apache-2.0), nor its
    trf.proto result encoding.

flowsdn test bodies are written from docs/spec/02-datapath-programs.md (the
behaviour under test) and docs/spec/18-bpf-test-harness.md (the harness),
following the clean-room protocol in docs/licensing.md. The harvested case
name and shape serve as the checklist entry, not as the implementation.

Re-harvesting
-------------

  python3 tools/harvest-bpf-cases.py tests/bpf/CASES.toml

with a read-only checkout of the reference at ../cilium. Bumping the reference
tag is a deliberate, reviewed operation (ADR-0005); the diff of CASES.toml is
exactly the set of cases the bump added, removed or renamed.

NOTICE roll-up
